{"service_name" : "email-service"}} 3 {"type" : "relationship" , "id" : "0" , "label" : "COMM1" , " start" : {"id" : "37550" , "labels" : [ "FES" ] , } , "end" that partition using the Listing 9. 1 result = config graph . similarity partition configuration”) search (”frontend : {"id" : "37551" , "labels" : [ "ES" ] , } , "properties" Listing 9. AA1 focusing on partition frontend-partition queries the : {"action" : "SYN" , "constraint" : "deny" , "id" : "6 ec4f95c-f4e3-4516-92c1-172cec275696"}} IRSKG embeddings information for system configuration, f1. Listing 5. Neo4j IRSKG rule: frontend-service is denied CONNECT action to email-service. Nodes id=27751 (frontend-service) and id=27750 (email-service) are linked by a COMMUNICATES_TO edge (id=0) with a deny constraint. 1 {"type" : "node" , "id" : "2891" , "labels" : [ "IP1" ] , " properties" : {"ip_address" : "192.168.1.100" , " count" : 2}} 2 {"type" : "node" , "id" : "2892" , "labels" : [ "IP2" ] , " properties" : {"ip_address" : "192.168.1.101" , " count" : 2}} Listing 6. Neo4j IRSKG model input for vertices IP1 (192.168.1.100) and IP2 (192.168.1.101), each appearing twice in Graylog (Listing 4), yielding P(Vip=count ) = 2 in the count property per Equation 3a. 1 {"type" : "relationship" , "id" : "878" , "label" : " COMMUNICATES_TO" , "start" : {"id" : "2891" , "labels" : [ "IP1" ] , "properties" : {"ip_address" : " 192.168.1.100"}} ,"start" : { . . . , "properties" : {" ip_address" : "192.168.1.101"}} ,"properties" : {" count" : 2 , "id" : "6ec4f95c-f4e3-4516-92c2-172 cec275696"}} Listing 7. Neo4j IRSKG model input for edge E1,2 between IP1 (192.168.1.100) and IP2 (192.168.1.101), both appearing twice in Graylog (Listing 3), yielding P(E1,2p=count ) = 2 in the count property per Equation 3b. 1) Online Boutique Embedding Knowledge Graph: Embeddings are created and stored (also know as vector store) for the entire IRSKG, G, consisting of vertices, Vi, and edges, Ei,j, as shown in Figure 1 in the ‘Knowledge Extraction’ phase. For example, this can be done for G, using the LangChain API utilizing the OpenAI embedding. [51]. Alternatively, one could use the native Neo4j to store the graph itself [52]. We illustrate langchain Neo4j vector configuration information, f1 for the frontend-partition, shown in Figure 3 in the List 8. embedding_node_property has the P(V2) property names and node_label has the L1 value. 1 from langchain . . . import Neo4jVector OpenAIEmbeddings 2 ... 3 config graph = Neo4jVector . from existing graph ( 4 embedding=OpenAIEmbeddings () , 5 index name=‘‘ config index ” , 6 ... 7 t e x t n o d e p r o p e r t i e s =[ ”ram” , ”name” , . . . ] , 8 embedding node property=” config embedding ” ,) Listing 8. Sample OB system embedding of configuration, f1, for the frontend-partition. B. Intrusion Response System Large Language Model Tasks We demonstrated the IRSLLM tasks that follows the sequence diagram 2. An enterprise security analyst launches the IRSDAC in a web browser. The analyst uses the IRSDAC posts a query, Q1, ‘is there active breach in the system?’. IRSDAS uses a query template, T1, to populate the query and sends the query to the embedded database described in Section IV-A1 for each system partition agent. The template follows either multishot, zero-shot, and chain of thoughts techniques depending on availability of data to construct the templates. IRSDAS then fills another query templates, T2, with the returned IRSKG results from the IRS system partition agents, and posts it to the IRSLLM. IRSDA gathers the results from the agents and compose a tabular format response to the IRSDAC. In this case, it produces two results. The result in tabbed format, U1, ‘1) yes, frontend-service is compromised. 2) yes, frontendservice is attempting to connect to the email-service which is prohibited by the admin’. IRSDAC displays the results after formatting in the web browser. The analyst then poses a second query, Q2, ‘ Describe the rule that prohibits the connection.’. The IRSDA follows similar sequence flow and returns an answer with verbatim of the R1 with the associated listing 5. C. IRSLLM Fine Tuning Techniques IRSLLM delivers accurate, policy-aligned, and explainable responses by following the fine-tuning procedure described in Section III-D, which adapts the model to the organization’s operational environment and security requirements. Through this targeted fine-tuning, IRSLLM can interpret enterprise policies encoded in the IRSKG and reliably generate decisions, such as issuing a DENY response to unauthorized connection attempts according to the governing ROE (R1). In our OB breach scenario, this alignment enables IRSDA to contain the attack and restore the system to a healthy state, while seamless collaboration between IRSLLM and the IRSDAAO orchestration layer ensures all incident responses are transparent, traceable to enterprise policies, and readily actionable by analysts. D. IRSDAAO Agentic Brain Design 15 l o g g i n g a g e n t = Runner ( a g e n t =AA3 , app name=APP NAME, session service=session service ) The IRSDAAO module delivers orchestration and agentic 16 . . . intelligence throughout the IRSDA system, enabling seamless 17 c a l l a g e n t ( ” i s t h e r e a c t i v e b r e a c h i n t h e s y s t e m ? ” ) integration with the multi-agent system (MAS) framework Listing 10. Here is the sample of the general snippet code of how to design described in Section III-F. This implementation employs the the IRSDAAO using Google ADK notations. Python-based ADK [46], in which a root agent serves as the central agentic brain (AB) and coordinates three subordinate V. CONCLUSION AND FUTURE WORK sub agents (AA1, AA2, AA3) in a parent-child hierarchy to manage distributed workflows. The operational sequence is initiated when the root agent receives a user request from IRSDAS. This agent delegates the task to AA1, which is tasked with coordinating IRSAgent activities and retrieving information from partitioned knowledge sources within IRSKG. After collecting the relevant data, AA1 transfers these results to the AB, which then activates AA2. The second agent, AA2, synthesizes the information and communicates with IRSLLM to generate responses. These outputs are structured in an HTML format with multiple tabs, enhancing user navigation and clarity of presentation. The system maintains context and state across interactions using the session.state mechanism, while AA3 runs asynchronously to log telemetry and operational metadata, ensuring transparency and traceability throughout the IRSDA lifecycle. Through this orchestrated workflow, IRSDA is able to detect the breach in the frontend-service, enforce the relevant ROE, and generate timely, context-aware responses for containment. The integration of IRSKG for knowledge representation, IRSLLM for incident reasoning, and coordinated multi-agent execution ensures that the attack remains isolated and the system is promptly restored to a secure and healthy operational state. Even with modular design and compartmentalized communication, machine learning frameworks may still be compromised by adversaries [53]. To remain within the research scope, we do not address these adversarial scenarios. Therefore, it is crucial to ensure each component In this paper, we presented the Intrusion Response System Digital Assistant (IRSDA), a unified and modular framework that combines intrusion detection and automated response through the integration of agentic orchestration, partition-scoped IRS agents, a property-graph knowledge base, and a cybersecurity-tuned LLM. The system supports realtime, ROE-compliant decision-making by coordinating agents across system partitions and grounding their actions using enterprise knowledge and telemetry through RAG. IRSDA leverages the MAPE-K loop to deliver end-to-end cyber defense, from monitoring and detection to planning and execution. Our case study on a microservices-based system demonstrated that IRSDA can effectively retrieve context-specific evidence, generate explainable and policy-aligned recommendations, and contain threats without manual intervention. This highlights the value of combining LLM reasoning with agent-based control in security-critical environments. As a next step, we propose extending IRSDA by incorporating reinforcement learning into IRS agents to support adaptive response policies over time in response to evolving attack patterns. This could improve system agility while preserving safety constraints defined by organizational policies. By unifying detection, response, and explainability, IRSDA addresses key limitations in traditional IDS and IR workflows. As cyber threats grow in complexity and speed, frameworks like IRSDA represent a necessary evolution in automated, trustworthy, and scalable enterprise defense. is secure before deployment in real-world applications. The IRSDA platform thus demonstrates its ability to deliver adap- VI. ACKNOWLEDGMENT tive, explainable, and resilient defense across the enterprise This work was supported by the Predictive Analytics and environment. The next section presents the conclusion and Technology Integration (PATENT) Laboratory at the Depart- outlines promising directions for future research. ment of Computer Science and Engineering, The University 1 from google . adk . agents import Agent 2 ... 3 APP NAME = ”IRSDAAO” 4 SESSION ID = ” s e s s i o n c o d e e x e c a s y n c ” 5 ... 6 GEMINI MODEL = ” g e m i n i −2.0 − f l a s h ” 7 AA1 agent = Agent ( . . . 8 i n s t r u c t i o n =” ” ”You\ ’ r e a s p e c i a l i s t i n delegating task to IRSAgents . . . and receive and r e t u r n r e s u l t s b a c k t o AB . ” ” ” , 9 code executor=BuiltInCodeExecutor () ) 10 . . . 11 r o o t a g e n t = Agent ( . . . 12 t o o l s = [ a g e n t t o o l . A g e n t T o o l ( a g e n t =AA1) , a g e n t t o o l . A g e n t T o o l ( a g e n t =AA2) , a g e n t t o o l . A g e n t T o o l ( a g e n t =AA3) ] ) 13 s e s s